but customers here aren't only buying breakfast.

44
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:55,960
They're buying time.

45
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,720
We like to keep the customer between like a two-and-a-half-minute

46
00:02:59,721 --> 00:03:01,440
and three-and-a-half-minute transaction

47
00:03:01,441 --> 00:03:03,380
from the time they walk in to the time they leave.

48
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:04,760
Everybody's very fast here.

49
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:06,340
As soon as you get to the counter,

50
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,660
they ring you up and you're out of here.

51
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:10,560
No matter what it is, you're always out, in and out, so...

52
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:14,100
We take speed for granted at convenience stores,

53
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:19,060
But there's a science to moving customers in and out in three and a half minutes or less.

54
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:23,460
And if anyone knows how to do it, it's architect Joseph Bona.

55
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:29,120
He's been designing convenience stores for over 30 years and always begins at the same place.

56
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,820
The successful development of any store starts with the layout.

57
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:41,280
Regardless of a convenience store's size, the layout divides the space into three zones, and each has a specific purpose.

58
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:47,180
The first is at the store's entrance, which Bona calls the decompression zone.

59
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,040
This is the area where people need to just take a second,

60
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,920
get their bearings from the outside to the inside, and be able to see everything.

61
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:59,960
While it might not seem important, the first moments in the decompression zone

62
00:03:59,961 --> 00:04:02,540
can make or break the convenience store shopping experience.

63
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,700
People buy with their eyes, and they eat with their eyes.

64
00:04:06,701 --> 00:04:13,060
having the layout be presented in a way where it's easy for them to find things is really, really critical.

65
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:18,700
From the decompression zone, customers enter the next and most highly designed part of the store,

66
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:20,220
the impulse zone.

67
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:26,040
In this area are the things that every consumer that comes in that front door

68
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:28,520
is going to be exposed to on the way in and the way out.

69
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,600
Things like coffee, like bakery, like sandwiches.

70
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:35,460
We want those really up front. We want that to be in the main flow.

71
00:04:35,461 --> 00:04:37,400
we want people to see that all the time.

72
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,220
In convenience stores, the self-serve coffee island

73
00:04:41,221 --> 00:04:43,720
is a high-traffic area engineered for speed.

74
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:46,900
The East Patchogue 7-Eleven is no exception.

75
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,900
The store sells about 1,500 cups of coffee a day,

76
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:53,920
800 cups during the three-hour morning rush.

77
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:57,280
That's nearly 20% of its business.

78
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:00,580
The secret is to keep customers moving.

79
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,380
It's like a production line.

80
00:05:03,381 --> 00:05:06,280
Somebody comes in, they want to grab a cup,

81
00:05:06,281 --> 00:05:09,960
they want to be able to get their coffee, and then move away.

82
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:11,580
We want to clear them away from the coffee

83
00:05:11,581 --> 00:05:14,260
so the next guest can come in, grab a cup, and grab a coffee.

84
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:19,500
As the customers work through the impulse zone,

85
00:05:19,501 --> 00:05:20,940
they pass through aisles designed

86
00:05:20,941 --> 00:05:23,460
to induce additional purchases.

87
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:26,180
There's a lot of opportunity to impulse guests

88
00:05:26,181 --> 00:05:27,940
into buying something that they weren't thinking about

89
00:05:27,941 --> 00:05:30,040
when they came in the front door.

90
00:05:30,041 --> 00:05:32,760
One of the important things is putting like things together.

91
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:35,860
We want to take the thinking away from guests.

92
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:39,380
When people come in, what makes a convenience store fast and efficient

93
00:05:39,381 --> 00:05:41,980
is that we take some of that decision-making away.

94
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:42,800
It's easy.

95
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,400
I can get my coffee, I can grab a bagel, I can grab a muffin,

96
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,000
and it's all within arm's reach.

97
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,860
The last zone in the layout is on the periphery of the space

98
00:05:50,861 --> 00:05:53,480
and serves customers looking for specific merchandise.

99
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:56,120
It's called the destination zone.

100
00:05:56,121 --> 00:06:00,520
People do come in to a convenience store for particular things in mind.

101
00:06:00,521 --> 00:06:05,240
And it might be something like an aspirin, a gift card, a newspaper, for example.

102
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:11,520
When I get my morning paper, I don't necessarily have to have this up front in the most accessible place

103
00:06:11,521 --> 00:06:14,400
because I'm coming in specifically with this purchase in mind.

104
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,580
That's a planned purchase. I could have that in a little further area of the store

105
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:20,900
only because I'm going to go there anyway.

106
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:24,820
One mainstay in the destination zone is the beverage cooler.

107
00:06:24,821 --> 00:06:28,300
which is deliberately placed in the back of the store.

108
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:32,060
We're seeing a bigger trend in convenience stores

109
00:06:32,061 --> 00:06:34,080
where a third of our sales come from the cooler.

110
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:37,160
We like to put that at the furthest point away from the door,

111
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,900
primarily because that drives people through the store.

112
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:42,960
And as these destination zone shoppers

113
00:06:42,961 --> 00:06:44,340
navigate the store to the checkout,

114
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:46,900
they run a gauntlet of temptations.

115
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:51,680
Designers also direct traffic in other ways,

116
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:54,620
using tricks as simple as the placement of a door hinge.

117
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,820
to lead customers toward the impulse zone.

118
00:06:56,821 --> 00:06:58,820
If you think about the way a door is hinged

119
00:06:58,821 --> 00:07:01,820
and the body movement and the reaction of people to doors,

120
00:07:01,821 --> 00:07:04,820
it's important to know that there's subtle little movements

121
00:07:04,821 --> 00:07:07,820
that could cause people to walk one way or another way.

122
00:07:07,821 --> 00:07:10,820
So if I'm walking down an aisle in this direction

123
00:07:10,821 --> 00:07:15,820
and I open my door and I grab a drink and I walk away,

124
00:07:15,821 --> 00:07:18,820
I'm almost forced to move in the same direction

125
00:07:18,821 --> 00:07:20,820
in which I just came.

126
00:07:20,821 --> 00:07:23,820
Whereas if the door was hinged on this side,

127
00:07:23,821 --> 00:07:26,578
side and I open the door and I grab a drink.

128
00:07:26,579 --> 00:07:29,900
As I close the door, I'm more likely to take my first

129
00:07:29,901 --> 00:07:32,148
step in the opposite direction of which I came.

130
00:07:32,149 --> 00:07:34,540
So there's little things like that in which we use

131
00:07:34,541 --> 00:07:37,784
the cooler to help move people in a different direction.

132
00:07:37,785 --> 00:07:39,580
The end point of the customer's

133
00:07:39,581 --> 00:07:42,571
three and a half minute visit is the checkout counter.

134
00:07:42,572 --> 00:07:44,620
People who buy with their eyes face a

135
00:07:44,621 --> 00:07:49,107
final enticement, the hot food service and a bevy of impulse snacks.

136
00:07:49,108 --> 00:07:51,020
People here who might want to

137
00:07:51,021 --> 00:07:56,180
to buy a hot dog or a hot sandwich, you know, only natural that you might want to grab a

138
00:07:56,181 --> 00:07:58,008
bag of chips at the same time.

139
00:07:58,009 --> 00:08:01,480
And even as we go to the checkout counter, maybe a little

140
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:03,634
candy or a little something sweet on the way out.

141
00:08:03,635 --> 00:08:05,260
So these are all the little cues that

142
00:08:05,261 --> 00:08:09,200
we're trying to do to give people an extra little moment to think about another little

143
00:08:09,201 --> 00:08:14,001
purchase, get them enticed to make, to pick something else up.

144
00:08:14,002 --> 00:08:15,860
But getting customers to

145
00:08:15,861 --> 00:08:19,060
make another purchase is only part of the battle in the convenience store business.

146
00:08:19,061 --> 00:08:24,060
Keeping stores efficient and profitable means replacing inventory quickly.

147
00:08:24,061 --> 00:08:32,060
Recent developments in computer technology have produced a handheld device that cuts reordering time in half while making it more accurate.

148
00:08:32,061 --> 00:08:33,902
We do item by item management.

149
00:08:33,903 --> 00:08:37,464
We have a unit that's the merchandising ordering terminal.

150
00:08:37,465 --> 00:08:39,060
We refer to it as the MOT.

151
00:08:39,061 --> 00:08:48,060
With the MOT, employees can capture store inventory data and place resupply orders immediately without waiting until the end of the day or the week.

152
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:58,612
We take an inventory on what's on hand, we plug it into the unit, we make a forecast of what we think we're going to sell, and the Mott does the math and creates the

153
00:08:58,613 --> 00:08:59,480
order per item.

154
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:06,680
The East Patchogue 7-Eleven uses the Mott to do next-day ordering of its bakery, fresh food, and sandwich items.

155
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:10,560
The inventory is counted following the morning rush.

156
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:18,900
By 10 o'clock in the morning, our sales associate does the order for that, and it's put in and it's delivered between midnight and 1 in the morning.

157
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:24,060
The mod also allows convenience stores to tailor their inventory to customer preferences.

158
00:09:24,061 --> 00:09:29,060
The key is to stay in stock on top sellers, which keeps the customers coming back.

159
00:09:29,061 --> 00:09:34,060
The store is really designed to fit the neighborhood and what those customers want.

160
00:09:34,061 --> 00:09:40,060
So it's constantly changing, constantly deleting items and bringing new items in to see if they're going to work on it.

161
00:09:40,061 --> 00:09:46,060
Daily deliveries of new merchandise must be squeezed in between the store's busy periods.

162
00:09:46,061 --> 00:09:48,060
The average convenience store's inventory

163
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:51,460
includes about 5,000 individual products.

164
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:52,660
There's little room on the shelves

165
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:55,060
for items that don't sell quickly.

166
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:58,200
There's about 1,600 square feet of selling space here.

167
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,200
We don't have much back room storage.

168
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:03,520
So when they deliver the cases, we get them into the floor,

169
00:10:03,620 --> 00:10:04,920
into the back room.

170
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:07,420
When it gets too much, we bring them out to stock the shelves

171
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:09,720
also just to keep the flow of the customers.

172
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:16,060
In the East Patchogue 7-Eleven,

173
00:10:16,061 --> 00:10:19,740
the store layout and inventory management tools have paid off significantly.

174
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,180
Despite being one of the smaller stores in the 7-Eleven chain,

175
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:25,560
it ranks among the top in sales volume,

176
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:28,380
a marvel of a three-and-a-half-minute transaction.

177
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:35,520
In our rapid-paced, time-crunched world,

178
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:38,440
convenience stores are the pit stop for people on the go.

179
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:42,540
While two-thirds of sales come at the gas pump,

180
00:10:42,541 --> 00:10:44,540
It's pick-me-up meals, snacks, and beverages

181
00:10:44,541 --> 00:10:46,540
that produce big profits.

182
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:49,540
$5.78.

183
00:10:49,541 --> 00:10:51,540
But in just about every convenience store,

184
00:10:51,541 --> 00:10:54,540
you'll also find some longtime favorites.

185
00:10:54,541 --> 00:10:58,540
Classics, that draw customers back again and again.

186
00:10:58,541 --> 00:11:01,540
This frozen concoction is known by several names,

187
00:11:01,541 --> 00:11:04,540
but it's best identified by its signature sound.

188
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:09,540
Slurpee is 7-Eleven's name for this mixture

189
00:11:09,541 --> 00:11:13,160
11's name for this mixture of flavored syrup, water, and CO2 in a cup.

190
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:22,360
Over 6 billion have been sold since 1966, with sales topping $170 million a year in

191
00:11:22,361 --> 00:11:23,180
the U.S. alone.

192
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:27,080
But the amazing thing about this brain freeze producer is its origin.

193
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:32,420
It was invented by accident during the late 50s in a small Kansas convenience store.

194
00:11:32,421 --> 00:11:37,860
A gentleman by the name of Omar Koneklik had a fountain machine which broke on a hot summer

195
00:11:37,861 --> 00:11:41,160
day and he put some bottles of Coca-Cola in a freezer.

196
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:48,160
What came out of that freezer was a slushy carbonated drink that his customers loved.

197
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:52,532
But today's Slurpee isn't the same as the original.

198
00:11:52,533 --> 00:11:55,240
A mid-90s change in dispenser technology

199
00:11:55,241 --> 00:11:58,170
ushered in a new era of the icy treat.

200
00:11:58,171 --> 00:12:02,180
The big innovation came when the machine was able to

201
00:12:02,181 --> 00:12:07,180
combined water with a special syrup that allowed the CO2 to absorb it.

202
00:12:07,181 --> 00:12:12,180
And that gives that creamy consistency, fluffy consistency.

203
00:12:12,181 --> 00:12:19,180
Making a perfect Slurpee time after time is a blend of science and technology.

204
00:12:19,181 --> 00:12:26,180
And it starts here at FBD, Frozen Beverage Dispensers, in San Antonio, Texas.

205
00:12:26,181 --> 00:12:30,180
On the plant floor, dispensers are assembled one by one.

206
00:12:30,181 --> 00:12:35,860
Over a thousand separate parts go into making each machine, considered a major advance over

207
00:12:35,861 --> 00:12:37,860
the previous version.

208
00:12:37,861 --> 00:12:43,300
The evolution of electronics, of sensor technology has allowed us to develop a whole new piece

209
00:12:43,301 --> 00:12:44,300
of equipment here.

210
00:12:44,301 --> 00:12:47,900
It's basically a chemical plant in a box.

211
00:12:47,901 --> 00:12:53,740
A mixing module pumps water, syrup and CO2 into the dispenser, where they're blended together.

212
00:12:53,741 --> 00:12:59,220
Then, the mixture is fed into one of several 18-inch chilled aluminum barrels housed within

213
00:12:59,221 --> 00:13:00,935
the machine's cold chamber.

214
00:13:00,936 --> 00:13:05,000
The icy mixture pours from the dispenser at temperatures between

215
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:12,560
24 and 28 degrees, with the sugar and the syrup acting as an anti-freezing agent. An

216
00:13:12,561 --> 00:13:18,100
electronic circuit board controls the dispenser's operation.

217
00:13:18,101 --> 00:13:25,220
We have sensors throughout this machine that are monitoring various elements of flow, heat

218
00:13:25,221 --> 00:13:30,180
transfer and those sensors then are feeding information to a very sophisticated computer

219
00:13:30,181 --> 00:13:31,180
program.

220
00:13:31,181 --> 00:13:36,340
The computer monitors the mixture inside the barrels for its ice content, while a rotating

221
00:13:36,341 --> 00:13:39,780
dasher stirs the product to keep it mixed.

222
00:13:39,781 --> 00:13:44,060
The challenge in making that perfect product is that we want a smooth, creamy texture when

223
00:13:44,061 --> 00:13:45,580
it comes out of here.

224
00:13:45,581 --> 00:13:49,980
That means the exact ice content has to be maintained.

225
00:13:49,981 --> 00:13:53,660
And usually that's about 60% ice.

226
00:13:53,661 --> 00:13:58,660
Since 1996, FBD has manufactured over 40,000 frozen drink dispensers.

227
00:13:58,661 --> 00:14:04,660
Today, our equipment is in just about every 7-Eleven store in the United States.

228
00:14:04,661 --> 00:14:10,660
Frozen drinks, slurpy drinks are fun, and that's what gets people coming back.

229
00:14:10,661 --> 00:14:17,660
Omar Knedlik gets the credit for creating this worldwide, multi-million dollar convenience store craze.

230
00:14:17,661 --> 00:14:22,920
But the idea for the convenience store belongs to Johnny Green of Dallas, Texas,

231
00:14:22,921 --> 00:14:26,540
who in 1927 ran a Southland Company ice house.

232
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:33,320
These were retail stores that sold blocks of ice to customers before in-home refrigerators became widely available.

233
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:38,200
Green's store was open every day, 7 in the morning to 11 at night,

234
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:43,340
and like other ice houses, was also a gathering place for people looking to beat the heat during the summer.

235
00:14:43,341 --> 00:14:52,680
Green began offering milk, bread and eggs to customers after local grocery stores were closed and the convenience store concept was born.

236
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:56,720
Over time, that concept spread to other Southland ice houses.

237
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:05,080
By 1946, the company named its chain of stores 7-Eleven, referring to their hours of operation.

238
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:12,780
Other convenience stores sprang up, too, like Weigel's, Quicktrek and Wawa.

239
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:16,980
often in areas too small or remote to support a large supermarket.

240
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:21,040
The idea was not to compete with a supermarket,

241
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:24,400
but to be convenient and speedy,

242
00:15:25,360 --> 00:15:30,320
carrying a broad range of staples and fresh products,

243
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:32,140
but not in lots of sizes.

244
00:15:32,760 --> 00:15:35,760
By the mid-1960s, the convenience store industry

245
00:15:35,761 --> 00:15:38,500
topped a billion dollars in sales for the first time.

246
00:15:38,501 --> 00:15:41,020
A decade later, it reached $10 billion.

247
00:15:41,021 --> 00:15:48,980
Today, sales total over a half trillion dollars, largely due to gasoline purchases and a steady

248
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:54,720
roster of snacks that have been engineered to be fast, cheap, and good, including a convenience

249
00:15:54,721 --> 00:15:56,770
store favorite, beef jerky.

250
00:15:56,771 --> 00:16:01,780
This ready-to-eat snack is enjoyed by hikers, campers, and hunters

251
00:16:01,781 --> 00:16:05,187
for its high-protein, low-fat content.

252
00:16:05,188 --> 00:16:09,940
A popular brand is Jack Link's, available in numerous

253
00:16:09,941 --> 00:16:10,641
varieties.

254
00:16:10,642 --> 00:16:15,540
The Link family has been in the meat packing and livestock business for generations.

255
00:16:16,980 --> 00:16:22,020
They started producing beef jerky in 1995 and meat snacks a few years earlier.

256
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:27,620
Drying is the oldest method of preserving food.

257
00:16:27,621 --> 00:16:30,980
In the 1800s, beef jerky was a staple of Native

258
00:16:30,981 --> 00:16:35,860
Americans and pioneers who relied on it to last through the winter months when food was scarce.

259
00:16:35,861 --> 00:16:39,860
People today still make jerky for personal use.

260
00:16:39,861 --> 00:16:43,860
But large-scale production for the consumer market poses challenges.

261
00:16:43,861 --> 00:16:49,860
The number one key challenge to making beef jerky is making sure that it will stand up to 18 months' worth of shelf life.

262
00:16:49,861 --> 00:16:52,860
That calls for relentless quality control.

263
00:16:52,861 --> 00:16:58,860
The company's jerky starts with premium lean beef in 2,000-pound vats.

264
00:16:58,861 --> 00:17:02,860
On average, one vat will yield 5,000 packages of jerky.

265
00:17:02,861 --> 00:17:05,200
The meat is injected with various marinade mixtures

266
00:17:05,201 --> 00:17:06,860
for flavoring.

267
00:17:06,861 --> 00:17:10,000
Then it's transferred into large bins of curing solution.

268
00:17:10,001 --> 00:17:12,460
It'll remain there from 6 to 72 hours,

269
00:17:12,461 --> 00:17:15,300
depending on the flavor of the jerky.

270
00:17:15,301 --> 00:17:18,620
After the marination process, we'll slice the product

271
00:17:18,621 --> 00:17:23,020
and place it onto rods for prep to the smokehouse.

272
00:17:23,021 --> 00:17:24,860
Here in stainless steel lockers,

273
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:27,300
they use smoke-enhanced heat to cook the strips

274
00:17:27,301 --> 00:17:29,320
from three to nine hours.

275
00:17:29,321 --> 00:17:32,020
During cooking, the meat reaches an internal temperature

276
00:17:32,021 --> 00:17:35,500
of 160 degrees to prevent the growth of harmful bacteria.

277
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:38,520
Technicians in a nearby control room

278
00:17:38,521 --> 00:17:40,660
monitor the temperature inside the smokehouse.

279
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:44,820
After cooking, the jerky moves to the packing machine area.

280
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:47,560
Right here, you can see the product

281
00:17:47,561 --> 00:17:50,240
in the vibratory conveyor, and we have an inspector

282
00:17:50,241 --> 00:17:51,980
on the line inspecting the product

283
00:17:51,981 --> 00:17:53,520
before we go into the package.

284
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:57,560
He's looking for any defects in the product.

285
00:17:58,540 --> 00:18:01,320
Following inspection, the jerky goes to a weighing machine.

286
00:18:01,321 --> 00:18:03,860
Then it's packed in vacuum sealed bags.

287
00:18:03,861 --> 00:18:07,560
To assure the jerky will stay fresh up to 18 months,

288
00:18:07,561 --> 00:18:10,920
an oxygen absorption packet is inserted into each bag.

289
00:18:10,921 --> 00:18:15,960
If you don't have that in there, you run the risk of possibly

290
00:18:15,961 --> 00:18:18,260
having trapped oxygen within that bag,

291
00:18:18,261 --> 00:18:20,320
and then your product could potentially mold.

292
00:18:20,321 --> 00:18:22,920
Lastly, as a quality control measure,

293
00:18:22,921 --> 00:18:25,920
the jerky is sealed in the bag with a nitrogen preservative.

294
00:18:25,921 --> 00:18:30,560
From this point, these packages are going to travel out

295
00:18:30,561 --> 00:18:34,220
to be boxed into a case that will ultimately end up at the convenience store.

296
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:41,380
Beef jerky's reliability as a safe, protein-rich snack

297
00:18:41,381 --> 00:18:43,500
has made it popular with people on the go.

298
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:47,860
But its biggest fans are out-of-this-world pioneers,

299
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:49,700
America's astronauts,

300
00:18:49,701 --> 00:18:53,160
who take jerky along on space flights as a lightweight pick-me-up.

301
00:18:53,161 --> 00:19:01,300
They come for a fast cup of coffee.

302
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:03,280
For a quick bite.

303
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:06,160
But mainly, they come to refuel.

304
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:11,500
Americans pour more than $500 billion a year into convenience store cash registers.

305
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:15,260
Nearly two-thirds comes from gas purchases,

306
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:18,500
sales that net less than $0.04 per gallon in profit.

307
00:19:18,501 --> 00:19:23,500
It's led convenience stores to try tapping into the customer's wallet another way.

308
00:19:23,501 --> 00:19:29,500
We're seeing a really big increase in food service.

309
00:19:29,501 --> 00:19:31,500
More and more people are coming to convenience stores to eat.

310
00:19:31,501 --> 00:19:35,500
It's going to continue to be more about feeding people.

311
00:19:35,501 --> 00:19:39,500
Now we have a brand new hot food program, which is just fantastic.

312
00:19:39,501 --> 00:19:43,500
It allows customers to come in, pick up lunch, dinner.

313
00:19:43,501 --> 00:19:45,500
They could always have breakfast here.

314
00:19:45,501 --> 00:19:48,360
have a pizza in 90 seconds hot and outdoor,

315
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:51,200
chicken tenders and the sortment of chicken wings.

316
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:54,980
The battle for the eating-out dollar is heating up.

317
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:57,280
And the machine that helped bring this about?

318
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:01,240
A countertop rapid-cook oven called a Turbo Chef.

319
00:20:02,380 --> 00:20:05,500
The Turbo Chef cooks food up to 12 times faster

320
00:20:05,501 --> 00:20:06,520
than a conventional oven.

321
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:10,380
In the convenience store, that speed translates into sales.

322
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:13,200
What it does for the convenience store operator

323
00:20:13,201 --> 00:20:15,760
is it enables them to serve foods

324
00:20:15,761 --> 00:20:18,780
that they normally wouldn't get in a quick-service manner.

325
00:20:20,540 --> 00:20:22,280
Pizzas, wings, sandwiches,

326
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:25,740
all cook to order in less than about, you know, three minutes.

327
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:28,480
The Turbo Chef manufacturing plant

328
00:20:28,481 --> 00:20:30,220
is located near Dallas, Texas,

329
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:32,920
where they produce nine types of ovens

330
00:20:32,921 --> 00:20:35,020
for a range of rapid-cooking platforms.

331
00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:37,860
The patented technology behind them

332
00:20:37,861 --> 00:20:40,100
combines a hairdryer-type heat source

333
00:20:40,101 --> 00:20:42,060
with a conventional microwave oven.

334
00:20:42,061 --> 00:20:45,520
Microwave ovens use radio frequency energy

335
00:20:45,521 --> 00:20:48,260
to vibrate the water molecules inside food

336
00:20:48,261 --> 00:20:49,480
in order to heat it,

337
00:20:49,720 --> 00:20:51,860
but the ovens don't provide consistent quality

338
00:20:51,861 --> 00:20:53,680
for the commercial food service industry.

339
00:20:54,440 --> 00:20:56,880
The Turbo Chef oven uses high-velocity,

340
00:20:57,020 --> 00:20:59,260
high-temperature heat to cook the surface of the food

341
00:20:59,261 --> 00:21:01,220
while the microwaves cook the inside.

342
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,340
The heat is circulated evenly throughout the chamber

343
00:21:04,341 --> 00:21:06,320
through jets above and below the food.

344
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:09,760
The original concept was developed in the mid-'90s

345
00:21:09,761 --> 00:21:11,580
for the fast-food pizza market.

346
00:21:12,060 --> 00:21:15,220
But cooking 10 times faster brought a problem.

347
00:21:15,221 --> 00:21:17,860
The oven would produce 10 times the amount of grease,

348
00:21:17,861 --> 00:21:19,100
smoke, and odor.

349
00:21:21,100 --> 00:21:24,100
The solution came from a surprising place,

350
00:21:24,101 --> 00:21:25,940
a car's exhaust system.

351
00:21:25,941 --> 00:21:28,740
This catalytic filtration is very much the same

352
00:21:28,741 --> 00:21:32,820
as what's found in every automobile in the world today.

353
00:21:32,821 --> 00:21:36,420
Basically, in our oven, as the air impacts the food,

354
00:21:36,421 --> 00:21:39,580
it picks up the odor, the smoke, and the grease

355
00:21:39,581 --> 00:21:41,620
that's been generated from the food item.

356
00:21:41,621 --> 00:21:44,680
It passes through the body of the catalytic converter,

357
00:21:45,160 --> 00:21:47,660
and that's then converted into CO2 and water,

358
00:21:48,180 --> 00:21:50,860
and then that clean air is then recirculated through the oven.

359
00:21:51,220 --> 00:21:54,420
The catalytic filters solved Turbo Chef's cooking problem,

360
00:21:54,780 --> 00:21:56,900
but the commercial food service industry

361
00:21:56,901 --> 00:21:58,900
was slow to accept rapid-cook ovens.

362
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:04,080
Then, in 2003, the Subway chain adopted the Turbo Chef oven

363
00:22:04,081 --> 00:22:05,480
for its hot-toasted sandwiches.

364
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:09,180
From 1993 to about 2003,

365
00:22:09,181 --> 00:22:13,920
we probably developed and sold close to 3,000 ovens.

366
00:22:14,100 --> 00:22:21,120
In 2004, we rolled out about 19,000 ovens to Subway for their new toasting initiative.

367
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:25,700
It didn't take long for convenience stores to jump on the rapid-cook bandwagon.

368
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:31,740
With whole pizzas ready in 90 seconds and toasted sandwiches in less than half a minute,

369
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:35,860
Turbo Chef has become a fixture on convenience store counters nationwide.

370
00:22:35,861 --> 00:22:42,340
We are in a lot of the major convenience stores, a lot of the major chains.

371
00:22:42,341 --> 00:22:49,980
We have about 90,000 ovens installed worldwide in about 93 countries.

372
00:22:49,981 --> 00:22:54,820
The Rapid Cook oven is changing the kinds of foods convenience stores offer their customers.

373
00:22:54,821 --> 00:22:59,100
But technology is also speeding up the ordering process.

374
00:22:59,101 --> 00:23:04,420
Case in point, Wawa convenience stores have placed computer terminals at their deli counters.

375
00:23:04,421 --> 00:23:06,420
Have a good one, buddy.

376
00:23:06,421 --> 00:23:10,420
For a team that makes 70 million sandwiches a year,

377
00:23:10,421 --> 00:23:13,420
this means faster sandwiches with fewer mistakes.

378
00:23:13,421 --> 00:23:16,420
That's a main menu. It's a simple touch screen.

379
00:23:16,421 --> 00:23:19,420
You can pick them from a menu of choices of snacks,

380
00:23:19,421 --> 00:23:21,420
beverages, breakfast, lunch, and dinner.

381
00:23:21,421 --> 00:23:23,420
And if we pick a lunch category,

382
00:23:23,421 --> 00:23:25,420
you can pick from many different selections for sandwiches,

383
00:23:25,421 --> 00:23:28,420
whether it be a toasted hoagie or a cold hoagie

384
00:23:28,421 --> 00:23:29,420
or a simple sandwich.

385
00:23:29,421 --> 00:23:32,420
But what's the customer's reaction?

386
00:23:32,421 --> 00:23:39,620
It's better than saying, hey, I want pepperoni on a toasted bologna sandwich with anything just you're built to order.

387
00:23:40,220 --> 00:23:41,940
In this case, you're a lot faster.

388
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:44,640
Actually, I tried to time myself.

389
00:23:45,240 --> 00:23:47,080
Fastest I ever got was three and a half seconds.

390
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:50,600
Time to see just how fast ordering a sandwich can be.

391
00:23:51,300 --> 00:23:55,860
Okay, so I hit my lunch menu, because that's where I get most of my wild hoagies at lunch.

392
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:57,220
Then I hit the cold hoagie.

393
00:23:57,320 --> 00:23:59,400
Once I hit cold hoagie, that's when it all begins.

394
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:00,580
All right, ready?

395
00:24:00,581 --> 00:24:02,420
One, two, three, go.

396
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:11,420
And that is my Wawa Hoagie.

397
00:24:11,421 --> 00:24:13,420
In the convenience store's ongoing quest

398
00:24:13,421 --> 00:24:16,420
to maintain customers, saving time by shaving seconds

399
00:24:16,421 --> 00:24:19,420
off any process is critical.

400
00:24:19,421 --> 00:24:21,420
But buying gas or a hot meal aren't the only reasons

401
00:24:21,421 --> 00:24:23,420
people stop at convenience stores.

402
00:24:23,421 --> 00:24:26,420
For some, it's about trying your luck.

403
00:24:26,421 --> 00:24:29,420
Well, we come for the lottery.

404
00:24:29,421 --> 00:24:31,420
Everybody wants to win.

405
00:24:31,421 --> 00:24:33,420
I just come for the lottery, that's it.

406
00:24:33,421 --> 00:24:35,420
You never know if you're gonna be the next lucky winner.

407
00:24:35,421 --> 00:24:37,420
And you can't win without a ticket.

408
00:24:37,421 --> 00:24:39,420
The urge to get rich quick

409
00:24:39,421 --> 00:24:41,420
has made winners of convenience stores.

410
00:24:41,421 --> 00:24:44,420
At least $30 billion is spent on lottery tickets

411
00:24:44,421 --> 00:24:47,420
in stores nationwide each year.

412
00:24:47,421 --> 00:24:50,420
But there's one place that's the jackpot capital of America.

413
00:24:50,421 --> 00:24:53,420
It's not Vegas or Atlantic City.

414
00:24:53,421 --> 00:24:55,420
It's here.

415
00:24:55,421 --> 00:24:57,420
Fond du Lac, Wisconsin.

416
00:24:57,421 --> 00:25:01,500
The town that sells the most winning lottery tickets in the U.S.

417
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:10,440
Winners worth over $500 million have been purchased at five stores along a stretch of road running through town called the Miracle Mile.

418
00:25:11,080 --> 00:25:13,300
And the luckiest place in America's luckiest town?

419
00:25:14,220 --> 00:25:15,800
Ma and Pa's convenience store.

420
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:18,700
Open 17 hours a day, seven days a week.

421
00:25:18,940 --> 00:25:22,880
A store where three lottery machines work nonstop from open to close.

422
00:25:22,881 --> 00:25:27,100
This store sells about a million and a half tickets a year on a normal basis.

423
00:25:27,101 --> 00:25:29,660
It's probably every minute that we're selling somebody a ticket.

424
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:32,980
There are usually about 30 games going on at a time,

425
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:37,380
from small scratch-off cards to the big multi-state lottery, like Power Bowl.

426
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:40,600
The bigger the prize, the busier Ma and Paz gets.

427
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:43,480
It's crazy when the lottery jackpot gets big.

428
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:48,400
Our record of selling tickets in one day was 95,000 tickets.

429
00:25:49,620 --> 00:25:53,360
That's over 6,000 tickets an hour, nearly two every second.

430
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:56,740
When the jackpot is really big, the place is packed.

431
00:25:56,741 --> 00:26:00,420
I mean, people would be outside the doors, people have to park blocks away,

432
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:03,540
and they just, they come in, and the place, it's just, it's a zoo.

433
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:10,020
Ma and Paz has sold over $237 million in winning lottery tickets since 1994.

434
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:12,500
Eight people have won $100,000 or more.

435
00:26:13,220 --> 00:26:14,820
Four have topped a million.

436
00:26:15,580 --> 00:26:18,960
The biggest prize went to 100 local cheese factory workers

437
00:26:18,961 --> 00:26:25,020
who shared a winning ticket worth over $208 million in 2006.

438
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:29,660
It's made the store a gathering place for players sharing one conviction.

439
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:31,060
It's a lucky place.

440
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:32,620
Luck gravitates here.

441
00:26:33,060 --> 00:26:34,540
I'm hoping it'll be lucky again.

442
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:37,160
Thank you.

443
00:26:37,240 --> 00:26:38,280
Is this the winning number?

444
00:26:38,440 --> 00:26:39,140
You bet it is.

445
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:43,300
With so many winning tickets sold, is there a secret to lottery success?

446
00:26:43,940 --> 00:26:50,060
The statistics say that 65% of lottery winners are from QuickPix,

447
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:51,100
let the computer pick.

448
00:26:51,480 --> 00:26:54,560
The other 35% are people that choose their own numbers.

449
00:26:54,561 --> 00:26:57,800
so I guess the computer is the one that is lucky.

450
00:26:58,460 --> 00:27:00,760
I'm realistic about my chances of winning here,

451
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:02,680
so we're pretty much just messing around.

452
00:27:03,100 --> 00:27:06,580
I haven't won yet, but I still keep on trying.

453
00:27:09,360 --> 00:27:11,760
Ma and Paz has become the convenience store mecca

454
00:27:11,761 --> 00:27:14,540
for players armed with hope, a dollar, and a dream

455
00:27:14,541 --> 00:27:17,080
of adding their name to the store's list of winners.

456
00:27:17,081 --> 00:27:29,840
There are more than 144,000 convenience stores in the United States, most open 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

457
00:27:31,080 --> 00:27:35,000
These high-volume, high-speed dynamos generate tremendous cash flow.

458
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:40,100
But having all that cash on hand has a downside. It makes them targets.

459
00:27:40,101 --> 00:27:46,220
While annually, 80% of all convenience stores remain crime-free,

460
00:27:46,221 --> 00:27:49,860
across the nation, 60 stores are robbed each day.

461
00:27:50,320 --> 00:27:53,700
That makes security a critical concern for store owners and operators.

462
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:56,980
Stopping robberies begins with the store's design.

463
00:27:57,500 --> 00:28:02,080
We want people to see inside, and so, you know, having a lot of windows is really important.

464
00:28:03,220 --> 00:28:08,840
Hutch's Convenience Store in Elk City, Oklahoma, is a regional bus stop, handling up to 10 buses a day.

465
00:28:08,841 --> 00:28:13,840
To keep the store safe, open windows are a part of its security strategy.

466
00:28:13,841 --> 00:28:18,840
We put glass, as much glass as we can, so that everybody can see in and see out.

467
00:28:18,841 --> 00:28:21,840
We want everybody to be aware of what's going on.

468
00:28:21,841 --> 00:28:25,840
Anywhere in the store, you want to keep a line of sights open.

469
00:28:25,841 --> 00:28:33,840
Another way to improve security, putting the checkout counter near the front of the store, in full view of the outside.

470
00:28:33,841 --> 00:28:39,080
But there's a high-tech level of protection, too, that takes a page from the world of spies.

471
00:28:40,480 --> 00:28:45,180
Companies like West Tech Intelligence Surveillance provide interactive store monitoring,

472
00:28:45,700 --> 00:28:48,280
streamed via the Internet to a remote command center.

473
00:28:48,740 --> 00:28:51,880
It's a technological advance over having an armed guard on site.

474
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:54,760
Really what you do with a guard is you project a sense of,

475
00:28:55,280 --> 00:28:57,360
well, this is probably not a safe place because they have a guard here.

476
00:28:57,840 --> 00:29:00,300
Why is there a guy walking around with a gun on his hip?

477
00:29:00,301 --> 00:29:02,480
This is probably not a place I want to do business.

478
00:29:02,481 --> 00:29:07,980
Hutch's convenience store employs an interactive system of high-resolution cameras, monitors,

479
00:29:07,981 --> 00:29:11,720
microphones and speakers to provide full store security coverage.

480
00:29:11,721 --> 00:29:16,520
With the cameras that we have in place here, with the presentation view monitor here, you

481
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:20,940
can announce your presence very subtly that says, we're watching.

482
00:29:20,941 --> 00:29:25,180
Watching and ready to activate the system at the first sign of trouble.

483
00:29:25,181 --> 00:29:30,800
All it takes is a push of a button to send a silent signal off-site.

484
00:29:30,801 --> 00:29:33,900
And that lands on a series of servers in our command center.

485
00:29:33,901 --> 00:29:37,240
And we take that signal that tells us exactly what's going on.

486
00:29:37,241 --> 00:29:38,660
Everything cold and secured.

487
00:29:38,661 --> 00:29:41,840
They can determine how serious it is,

488
00:29:41,841 --> 00:29:44,500
how quick the police need to respond.

489
00:29:44,501 --> 00:29:46,840
And they can also tell the individual

490
00:29:46,841 --> 00:29:49,080
that they need to leave the premises immediately.

491
00:29:49,081 --> 00:29:50,620
This is West Tech Interactive.

492
00:29:50,621 --> 00:29:53,480
Speaking directly to the individual in the red vest

493
00:29:53,481 --> 00:29:55,700
who is in the drive-through of this location,

494
00:29:55,701 --> 00:29:59,380
leave this property now or police will be dispatched.

495
00:29:59,381 --> 00:30:06,980
Digital technology also allows convenience store managers and owners to keep in touch with other stores, from a personal computer or mobile device.

496
00:30:07,460 --> 00:30:14,140
That's an advantage for Chris Stevens, who runs the East Patchogue 7-Eleven on Long Island, New York, as well as several other stores.

497
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:18,940
I'm in constant communication with all my stores, 24-7.

498
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:26,020
I'm also in visual contact with them by using my iPad and tapping into my security systems in all the stores.

499
00:30:26,021 --> 00:30:32,020
Through its layout and high-tech surveillance tools, a convenience store can send a clear message.

500
00:30:32,021 --> 00:30:42,020
With someone that's looking to rob a facility, they want to be able to come in undetected and get in and out as quick as possible.

501
00:30:42,021 --> 00:30:47,020
And what we're trying to do is show them that that's not possible in our facility.

502
00:30:47,021 --> 00:30:53,020
One key way to reduce the threat of robbery is at the checkout register.

503
00:30:53,021 --> 00:30:58,160
Our staff are trained to keep very, very little amount of cash in the drawer.

504
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:03,420
We have a safe system that allows them to drop the cash as soon as they take it in.

505
00:31:03,720 --> 00:31:06,740
This way they main a minimal amount of cash in the drawer.

506
00:31:08,440 --> 00:31:12,200
Today's safe is the cornerstone of a store's cash management system.

507
00:31:12,740 --> 00:31:15,860
No longer a metal-clad box hidden away in a back room.

508
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:21,600
It's a mini fortress designed to deter robbery and also speed transactions at the checkout.

509
00:31:21,601 --> 00:31:25,600
It's also smart enough to instantly detect counterfeit bills.

510
00:31:25,601 --> 00:31:29,600
Our safe we have in place here has two bill validators.

511
00:31:29,601 --> 00:31:36,600
The safe will read the denomination and the amount, so we always know exactly how much they inserted into the safe.

512
00:31:36,601 --> 00:31:41,600
That way they can keep the amount of money in their drawer at a very minimum.

513
00:31:41,601 --> 00:31:46,600
No question the new generation of convenience store safes is a defense against robbery.

514
00:31:46,601 --> 00:31:48,600
But just how secure is it?

515
00:31:48,601 --> 00:31:51,600
Well, this safe is over 1,000 pounds.

516
00:31:51,601 --> 00:31:54,600
It is bolted into the floor.

517
00:31:54,601 --> 00:31:58,600
This would be virtually impossible to take out.

518
00:32:00,600 --> 00:32:03,600
If safes like these can't be taken out,

519
00:32:03,601 --> 00:32:05,600
can a determined criminal break into it?

520
00:32:05,601 --> 00:32:09,600
Dunbar Armored manufactures a 250-pound

521
00:32:09,601 --> 00:32:13,600
solid-steel electronic safe, the CN2400,

522
00:32:13,601 --> 00:32:16,600
built for the convenience store industry.

523
00:32:16,601 --> 00:32:20,400
The company agreed to a test of its strength and durability

524
00:32:20,401 --> 00:32:21,400
at a firing range.

525
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:25,380
Dunbar even supplied two safe crackers for the job.

526
00:32:26,440 --> 00:32:29,300
We brought out various tools that criminals might use

527
00:32:29,301 --> 00:32:32,300
to try to break into our safe, a drill, a crowbar,

528
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:34,560
a sledgehammer, and a two-headed axe.

529
00:32:36,240 --> 00:32:38,020
I'm going to try to drill out the lock

530
00:32:38,021 --> 00:32:39,120
to get to the money compartment.

531
00:32:39,121 --> 00:32:48,300
It just made a little hole, not but a quarter of an inch deep.

532
00:32:48,301 --> 00:32:50,720
It's not getting anywhere near the mechanism.

533
00:32:50,721 --> 00:32:55,600
Next, Steve tries a crowbar.

534
00:32:55,601 --> 00:33:02,640
And of course, because the door is tightly fitted, there's no way to get in.

535
00:33:02,641 --> 00:33:05,600
Now it's Mike's turn, using a nine-pound sledgehammer.

536
00:33:05,601 --> 00:33:16,200
I've been beating on this thing here for a little bit,

537
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:18,560
and the worst I've done is knocked off a little bit of paint

538
00:33:18,561 --> 00:33:20,640
and messed up the sticker.

539
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,500
The other thing I can try is maybe to cut off the hinges with an axe.

540
00:33:30,420 --> 00:33:32,960
Mike's axe assault lasts for 10 minutes.

541
00:33:32,961 --> 00:33:43,200
progress so far it's still intact i guess the next thing we can try to do is shoot the safe

542
00:33:43,201 --> 00:33:53,280
but not with just some small caliber pistol this is an ak-47 probably not what a robber

543
00:33:53,281 --> 00:33:56,540
of a comedian store would use but we just want to show the toughness of the safe

544
00:33:56,541 --> 00:34:10,541
Time to check the results.

545
00:34:16,820 --> 00:34:19,320
Well, we still don't seem to have been very effective

546
00:34:19,321 --> 00:34:21,860
in doing anything other than some more cosmetic damage

547
00:34:21,861 --> 00:34:23,040
and just knocking off the handle.

548
00:34:23,640 --> 00:34:25,320
It's something that can easily be replaced.

549
00:34:25,321 --> 00:34:29,320
We've tried to pry it open with a crowbar.

550
00:34:29,321 --> 00:34:32,320
We've tried to drill through the lock.

551
00:34:32,321 --> 00:34:36,320
We've hit it with a sledgehammer.

552
00:34:36,321 --> 00:34:40,320
We've hit it with a two-sided axe.

553
00:34:40,321 --> 00:34:43,320
And now we've shot it with an AK-47.

554
00:34:43,321 --> 00:34:48,320
And the safe is still intact, and the money's safe.

555
00:34:48,321 --> 00:34:51,320
There's no way for a criminal to get into this safe.

556
00:34:51,321 --> 00:34:54,320
Unless, of course, you have the combination and a key.

557
00:34:55,320 --> 00:35:02,900
For nearly a century, convenience stores have been walk-in brick-and-mortar buildings,

558
00:35:03,400 --> 00:35:05,960
stocked with goods and staffed by clerks and cashiers.

559
00:35:06,860 --> 00:35:09,700
And it's grown into a huge industry nationwide.

560
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:13,040
There's 145,000 stores.

561
00:35:13,300 --> 00:35:16,840
They process 58 billion transactions every year.

562
00:35:17,640 --> 00:35:18,400
Thank you.

563
00:35:18,960 --> 00:35:22,920
One in every $23 is spent in a convenience store.

564
00:35:22,921 --> 00:35:26,760
But a company in Memphis, Tennessee,

565
00:35:26,761 --> 00:35:29,160
is changing the look of the convenience store

566
00:35:29,161 --> 00:35:32,800
and the entire shopping experience.

567
00:35:32,801 --> 00:35:37,160
Smart Mart offers the ultimate in convenience.

568
00:35:37,161 --> 00:35:40,100
Drive-through shopping.

569
00:35:40,101 --> 00:35:44,440
Customers begin at one of four shopping ports.

570
00:35:44,441 --> 00:35:48,180
They use a touch screen to place their order.

571
00:35:48,181 --> 00:35:52,920
On-screen icons appear, corresponding to aisles in a store.

572
00:35:52,921 --> 00:35:55,820
They select products from that aisle to fill their shopping cart.

573
00:35:57,500 --> 00:36:02,260
After making their selections, customers pay for the items with cash, credit, or debit card.

574
00:36:06,500 --> 00:36:12,660
Then comes some robotic wizardry featuring computers, motors, and conveyor belts.

575
00:36:13,900 --> 00:36:20,520
Inside the 53 by 8 and a half foot building, a computer signals mechanized dispensers to release the products.

576
00:36:20,521 --> 00:36:26,880
As the products are dispensed, they're dropped onto a central conveyor belt that runs in

577
00:36:26,881 --> 00:36:30,960
either direction from the front of the store to the back.

578
00:36:30,961 --> 00:36:35,440
At the end of the conveyor belt, the products are transferred to diverter conveyors that

579
00:36:35,441 --> 00:36:39,640
move items from one side of the building to the other into one of the four different order

580
00:36:39,641 --> 00:36:42,320
ports.

581
00:36:42,321 --> 00:36:48,360
The Smart Mart system can handle a variety of product types and sizes.

582
00:36:48,361 --> 00:36:51,800
from a small pack of razor blades to a gallon of milk

583
00:36:51,801 --> 00:36:55,320
or 12 packs of beverages.

584
00:36:55,321 --> 00:36:59,160
The system also checks IDs for alcohol purchases.

585
00:36:59,161 --> 00:37:01,920
Customers place their driver's license in a slot.

586
00:37:01,921 --> 00:37:03,840
A digital camera transmits the photo

587
00:37:03,841 --> 00:37:06,040
to an off-site call center, and there it's

588
00:37:06,041 --> 00:37:08,200
compared to the customer in the car.

589
00:37:08,201 --> 00:37:11,440
Excellent.

590
00:37:11,441 --> 00:37:13,800
Connecting the motors, conveyor belts, and dispensers

591
00:37:13,801 --> 00:37:17,360
together are about 60 miles of wires and cables,

592
00:37:17,361 --> 00:37:20,700
about the same quantity found in a modern passenger jet.

593
00:37:23,540 --> 00:37:25,860
The store's delivery system has also been engineered

594
00:37:25,861 --> 00:37:28,540
to prevent a common shopping nuisance.

595
00:37:28,541 --> 00:37:31,240
You don't want your potato chips to come out

596
00:37:31,241 --> 00:37:35,340
and then have a 12-pack of soft-grain strap on top of it.

597
00:37:35,341 --> 00:37:38,720
So we've assigned a fragility factor to each product.

598
00:37:38,721 --> 00:37:42,120
We dispense the ones that are stronger or more rugged first

599
00:37:42,121 --> 00:37:44,220
or that might tend to crush the others.

600
00:37:44,221 --> 00:37:46,480
So your soft-grain, your gallon of milk

601
00:37:46,481 --> 00:37:49,400
come out before your bread and your eggs would come out.

602
00:37:52,420 --> 00:37:54,280
Smart Mart's technology is impressive,

603
00:37:54,680 --> 00:37:57,140
but it's not the first time automation has been introduced

604
00:37:57,141 --> 00:37:58,240
into retail shopping.

605
00:37:59,780 --> 00:38:02,340
That distinction goes to Clarence Saunders,

606
00:38:02,341 --> 00:38:05,060
who founded the first self-service grocery store,

607
00:38:05,640 --> 00:38:07,620
Higgly Wiggly, in 1916.

608
00:38:08,740 --> 00:38:12,720
In 1937, Saunders opened the fully automated grocery store,

609
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:15,220
also in Memphis, called Key Doozle.

610
00:38:15,221 --> 00:38:19,220
Shoppers viewed the merchandise displayed in glass cabinets.

611
00:38:19,221 --> 00:38:22,220
They made their selections by inserting a special key

612
00:38:22,221 --> 00:38:24,220
into a labeled keyhole.

613
00:38:24,221 --> 00:38:27,220
That produced a perforation punched onto a ticker tape.

614
00:38:27,221 --> 00:38:30,220
The tape would be read by a machine at the cashier's desk,

615
00:38:30,221 --> 00:38:34,220
which sent electric signals by wires to the back room,

616
00:38:34,221 --> 00:38:37,220
where the items would slide down conveyor belts to be bagged.

617
00:38:37,221 --> 00:38:40,220
But as innovative as Key Doozle was,

618
00:38:40,221 --> 00:38:42,220
Saunders' concept failed.

619
00:38:42,221 --> 00:38:45,220
The existing technology could not meet the demand.

620
00:38:45,221 --> 00:38:47,220
Circuits overloaded.

621
00:38:47,221 --> 00:38:49,220
The conveyor belts couldn't keep up with the volume

622
00:38:49,221 --> 00:38:51,220
of purchases at peak times,

623
00:38:51,221 --> 00:38:54,220
and too often shoppers got the wrong merchandise.

624
00:38:54,221 --> 00:38:57,220
The three key doozle stores Saunders opened in Memphis

625
00:38:57,221 --> 00:38:59,220
closed by 1950,

626
00:38:59,221 --> 00:39:03,220
and his idea for a fully automated store went into mothballs

627
00:39:03,221 --> 00:39:05,220
until Smart Mart.

628
00:39:05,221 --> 00:39:09,220
Today, technology is in sync with the automated concept.

629
00:39:09,221 --> 00:39:14,320
Smart Mart is a store engineered to get you quickly back on the road.

630
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:20,360
But there's one convenience store in the Midwest where speed isn't the main attraction.

631
00:39:21,060 --> 00:39:28,120
Arcadia, Oklahoma is a small town of 300 people located along the mother road of America, Route 66.

632
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:34,040
For decades, motorists zoomed by Arcadia on their way west.

633
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:37,480
Now, they have a reason to stop.

634
00:39:37,481 --> 00:39:41,480
I like it because of the pop bottle and all the sodas.

635
00:39:41,481 --> 00:39:44,480
The coolest roadside landmark in America,

636
00:39:44,481 --> 00:39:49,480
a pop bottle with a straw towering 66 feet high over Route 66.

637
00:39:49,481 --> 00:39:52,480
That's awesome. It's pretty big. It's amazing.

638
00:39:52,481 --> 00:39:55,480
The store is called, appropriately enough, Pops.

639
00:39:55,481 --> 00:39:58,480
And since opening in 2007,

640
00:39:58,481 --> 00:40:01,480
it's become one of the most popular sites in Oklahoma.

641
00:40:03,480 --> 00:40:06,480
Inside, along with offering travelers food and fuel,

642
00:40:06,481 --> 00:40:08,480
Pops adds fizz.

643
00:40:08,481 --> 00:40:11,480
You'll find over 600 different flavors of soda

644
00:40:11,481 --> 00:40:14,480
from the U.S. and 17 countries around the world.

645
00:40:14,481 --> 00:40:19,480
I like the, um, uh, Purple Crush.

646
00:40:19,481 --> 00:40:21,480
I got some random flavors.

647
00:40:21,481 --> 00:40:22,480
She got some squirt.

648
00:40:22,481 --> 00:40:24,480
Swamp juice. Yeah.

649
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:29,480
Most of the sodas here, especially the stranger ones,

650
00:40:29,481 --> 00:40:31,480
the weird ones, they move well.

651
00:40:31,481 --> 00:40:32,480
People love them.

652
00:40:32,481 --> 00:40:34,480
We've got everything from cucumber sodas

653
00:40:34,481 --> 00:40:38,020
to celery sodas to sodas made with jalapeno oil,

654
00:40:38,021 --> 00:40:41,720
sodas made with dill weed, juniper berries, spruce beer,

655
00:40:41,721 --> 00:40:43,680
and then, of course, just a wide array

656
00:40:43,681 --> 00:40:46,120
of the various root beers, oranges, grapes,

657
00:40:46,121 --> 00:40:49,820
and a lot of the more standard flavors, mango, banana,

658
00:40:49,821 --> 00:40:51,440
you name it, it's out there.

659
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:54,840
Want to check on you? Make sure you're all right.

660
00:40:54,841 --> 00:40:56,840
Good, good, good, thank you.

661
00:40:56,841 --> 00:40:58,840
With a restaurant and a retail store

662
00:40:58,841 --> 00:41:00,740
selling a variety of souvenirs,

663
00:41:00,741 --> 00:41:03,640
Pops is blurring the definition of a convenience store.

664
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:10,398
What is Pops? Is it a gas station? Is it a convenience store? Is it a restaurant?

665
00:41:10,399 --> 00:41:11,640
Is it a landmark?

666
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:14,220
This is not just a stop, it's an experience.

667
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:19,280
An experience that for most customers lasts longer than a three and a half minute pit stop.

668
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:24,300
This is her birthday. She decided to do her birthday here.

669
00:41:25,020 --> 00:41:32,760
We kind of celebrate our 25th anniversary and they're getting married soon, so I thought we'd come and have some fun.

670
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:39,480
Pops is a convenience store that's about more than speed.

671
00:41:39,481 --> 00:41:44,480
We've become the place where people gather in this area.

672
00:41:44,481 --> 00:41:51,480
That's actually where the idea of the first convenience store began, in a Dallas, Texas ice house in 1927.

673
00:41:51,481 --> 00:41:57,480
It not only provided customers goods like milk and bread, it provided a sense of community.

674
00:41:57,481 --> 00:42:02,020
So while most convenience stores today are quick stops built for speed and efficiency,

675
00:42:02,900 --> 00:42:09,680
there are still places, like Pops, where people go not to buy time, but to spend it with each other.

